#import "headings.typ": H
= #H("ch5")

This research designed, implemented, and evaluated a hybrid Kubernetes-serverless architecture with GRU-based workload prediction for Kubernetes replica scaling and observed-load traffic routing. The system addresses elastic scalability management in heterogeneous cloud environments through capacity-aware traffic distribution and replica scaling. Three research questions were posed; each is answered below with direct reference to experimental evidence. The S3/S4 verdict rests on three ClarkNet-attributable paired batches (25 pairs). The July n = 5 batch (`2026-07-14_clarknet-tuned-paired-n5`) is retained as history.

== #H("ch5-conclusion")

=== #H("ch5-rq1")

A GRU (Gated Recurrent Unit) neural network was designed and tuned with a Tree-structured Parzen Estimator search under a leak-free chronological protocol. The selection chose two recurrent layers with 256 hidden units and a 30-sample input window. Head dropout is 0.055 and the learning rate is 0.000153, at a frozen budget of 23 epochs. Model selection used expanding-window folds that carry the same embargo as the training split. The replay window lies inside the test portion. Training never sees the traffic the model later serves.

On synthetic workload patterns the pre-registered target is met: RMSE 5.2% of the mean load and MAE 4.1%, against targets of 10% and 5% respectively. The network beats persistence by 21.9% on that arm.

On the real deployment trace the picture differs and must be stated plainly. The model trains on the amplified ClarkNet series. Scored on a held-out portion that contains the replay window, it reaches RMSE 29.6 RPS. That is 39.6% of the mean load, with skill 0.216 against persistence. The target is therefore not met on real traffic at 15-second resolution. A persistence forecast repeats the last observation. It already sits at 50.5%, so the task itself is hard at this granularity. The error is a property of the signal rather than of the network alone.

Two comparisons bound the architecture choice. Both networks trained under the identical protocol, same budget, same seeds. An LSTM reaches 29.917 and the GRU 29.635. The GRU is better on every seed, with a paired effect size of 1.86 and ten times lower seed variance. Three seeds give a one-sided permutation p of 0.125, the smallest value attainable. The advantage is consistent but not significant. Against a linear autoregression on the same 30-sample window, which reaches 29.465, the network holds no advantage. A twelve-lever representation search under the leak-free fold protocol confirms the bound: no variant beats the linear model on the selection folds (`results/models/gru/2026-09-13_probes-representation/`). GRU is preferable to LSTM by a small margin. At this resolution it matches a linear model. The deployed point predictor should therefore be the linear autoregression.

=== #H("ch5-rq2")

The research extends the ElaX framework @yang2019elax with two algorithms for heterogeneous cloud environments. Algorithm 1 (Routing Controller) splits traffic by capacity. Kubernetes carries load up to its effective capacity, which is ready replicas times the effective rate per replica. Serverless carries the burst above it, capped at 50% of traffic. A pacing term adds serverless weight if SLO violations use up their budget faster than allowed. It did not fire in any reported run. Each decision carries a label for statistics: PREDICTIVE, REACTIVE, RECOVERY, or MAINTAIN. Weight-based routing via HAProxy implements traffic distribution. The split ranges from 100/0 to 50/50.

Algorithm 2 (Cluster Controller) uses a horizontal scaling formula $R = alpha dot.op x + beta$. It integrates this formula into the routing daemon for real-time Kubernetes replica scaling. It operates in reactive mode (S3, using observed load) or predictive mode (S4, using the larger of the observed and the GRU forecast target).

Phase A1 validated the predictive pre-warming mechanism with the earlier priority-ladder controller. PREDICTIVE triggered at p99 = 146 ms (healthy state) when the observed-load trend indicated a predicted 47% workload surge and GRU confidence was 72%. It pre-positioned serverless capacity before an SLO violation occurred. In the V3 controller, the forecast informs Algorithm 2 replica planning. Routing weights follow observed load and ready capacity. In V3, PREDICTIVE labels a weight increase made while predicted_upper exceeds the observed load. The label does not change the split.

=== #H("ch5-rq3")

A comprehensive evaluation framework spans mechanism validation (Phase A1), comparative evaluation (Phase B), and dynamic burst validation (Phase C). The evaluation compares four scenarios: S1 (K8s+HPA), S2 (Serverless-only), S3 (Hybrid-reactive), and S4 (Hybrid-predictive). The Phase B baseline is an *n = 1 diagnostic* run per scenario (`2026-07-11_scaling_fix_n1`). The paired n = 5 July batch (`2026-07-14_clarknet-tuned-paired-n5`) reached significance but is not ClarkNet-attributable. The three ClarkNet-attributable batches (25 pairs) do not support H2.

H1 (hybrid architecture outperforms the pure Kubernetes baseline): *Directional at n = 1.* The infrastructure was correctly bounded (Docker `--cpus=1.0` per node, `max_k8s_replicas = 6`). Under it, the hybrid predictive scenario S4 reduced p99 latency by 95.1% (118.2 ms vs 2,421.3 ms for S1). It cut SLO violations by 98.5% (104 vs 6,717). The earlier negative finding was an artifact of un-enforced CPU limits and S1 dynamic-node over-provisioning (Bugs 8 and 11). The n = 5 four-scenario replication did not reproduce the advantage: with ten-replica headroom S1 did not saturate (S4 vs S1 −10.2%, p = 0.151).

H2 (predictive scaling outperforms reactive): *Not supported.* Across 25 ClarkNet pairs S4 ran 16.5 ms faster on average, and the 95% interval of the mean difference spans zero. One July batch (`2026-07-14_clarknet-tuned-paired-n5`) looked like a win (126.0 ms versus 188.5 ms, mean difference -62.5 ms, 95% CI [-100.9, -26.2], p = 0.0312, Cohen's d = -1.26). Its manifest records no predictor, and the artifact deployed then was the synthetic-trained model. The three ClarkNet batches since (5, 10, and 10 pairs, each recording artifact 20623c19) are all null: p = 0.656, p = 0.168, and p = 0.307. The last is a September repeat with host load recorded per run (-13.6 ms, 95% CI [-63.5, +35.6], d = -0.16, S4 winning 5 of 10 pairs). Pooling all 25 pairs gives -16.5 ms (95% CI [-188.6, +184.5], one-sided p = 0.448, d = -0.03). An interim subset (-101.3 ms, p = 0.039) did not survive the replication. The July batch's secondary results (SLO violations 130 vs 656, -80.2%; identical projected cost USD 163) and its 4--5 PREDICTIVE labels per S4 run remain descriptive history.

H3 (GRU prediction adequacy): *Met on synthetic data, not met on real traces.* Under the leak-free protocol the synthetic arm reaches RMSE 5.2% of the mean load and MAE 4.1%, against pre-registered targets of 10% and 5%. Scored on the held-out region of the deployment trace, the real deployment arm reaches RMSE 39.6% of the mean. Skill is 0.216 against persistence. The model matches a linear autoregression on the same input window rather than beating it. This is a disclosed limitation. A twelve-lever representation search ran under the leak-free fold protocol. No variant beats the linear model on the selection folds. Skill ranges from -0.01 percent (`robust_scale`) to -5.08 percent (`calendar`). The closest lever `nlinear` sits at -0.30 percent with a Diebold-Mariano p of 0.29. The network's parity is a limitation of this thesis, not a hidden fact. A capacity-relative crossing head detects crossings the point forecast misses. Recall is 0.988 $plus.minus$ 0.009 against 0.008 to 0.042 for the linear model plus threshold. The raw head is overconfident (per-step Brier 0.236), and isotonic calibration brings it to 0.043, under the linear reference of 0.049 (`results/models/gru/2026-09-13_probe-t-cross/`). Its decision rule is recall at a bounded false-alarm rate. The architecture comparison is our own: GRU 29.635 against LSTM 29.917 across three matched seeds, GRU better on every seed, paired effect size 1.86, one-sided permutation p 0.125.

The n = 1 diagnostic cost projection (S1 = USD 132/mo, S2 = USD 395/mo, S3 = USD 147/mo, S4 = USD 143/mo) is a *directional estimate only.* It must not rank S3 and S4 or claim monthly savings. S1 is cheapest but fails the SLO (p99 = 2,421 ms); S2 is fastest but most expensive; the hybrid scenarios occupy a middle band.

In summary, this research validates the hybrid mechanism: hybrid routing reduces p99 latency by 95.1% over saturated pure Kubernetes (H1, directionally supported at n = 1). The GRU predictor achieves target accuracy on synthetic data, and the routing controller shifts traffic based on observed load and ready capacity. For the predictive advantage over reactive control (H2), the replicated verdict is not statistically detectable. The only significant batch is not ClarkNet-attributable. All three ClarkNet-attributable batches are null on the pre-specified test. The pooled 25-pair estimate (-16.5 ms, 95% CI [-188.6, +184.5] ms) spans zero and is too wide to exclude effects of either sign up to about 180 ms. The three-tier autoscaling architecture (routing, pod scaling, and utilization-based node consolidation) is shown under realistic variable load. These findings confirm that the hybrid architecture improves tail-latency compliance over a saturated Kubernetes baseline. Whether the forecast adds detectable value over well-tuned reactive control remains open. The consolidation regime is the lead candidate where it can help.

A separate high-load diagnostic (`2026-07-13_dynamic-node-offload`, n = 1) shows the full three-tier architecture. It covers traffic routing (HAProxy weight shifting), pod scaling (Algorithm 2 / HPA), and node autoscaling (K3dAutoscaler dynamic node provisioning). Under 200 RPS constant load with a ten-replica cap, all Kubernetes scenarios provisioned two dynamic workload nodes via Pending-pod detection. The hybrid scenarios (S3, S4) maintained 96.5% serverless offload time while they provisioned nodes. They achieved p99 latency of 874 ms (S3) and 1,393 ms (S4). Pure Kubernetes (S1) had the same two dynamic nodes but no serverless offload. It achieved only 6,665 ms p99 and a 65.6% success rate. Dynamic node provisioning alone is not a substitute for serverless offload. The two mechanisms are complementary. This is a mechanism diagnostic, not an inferential experiment. Node-versus-serverless economics require the measured cost proxy and a larger replication study.

Under variable ClarkNet load with utilization-based node consolidation (`2026-07-14_clarknet-util-scaledown-n1`, n = 1), the predictive controller showed its strongest advantage. S4 achieved 50.5% lower p99 (113 ms vs 228 ms) and 96.3% fewer SLO violations (40 vs 1,085) than S3, at a 5% cost premium. The K3dAutoscaler's consolidation logic matched the Kubernetes Cluster Autoscaler's 50% utilization threshold and reschedulability check. It provisioned nodes on ClarkNet peaks and consolidated underutilized nodes on valleys. S3's aggressive consolidation deleted all dynamic nodes when utilization dropped. This caused latency spikes on the next peak. S4's forecast maintained capacity and prevented excessive consolidation.

== #H("ch5-future")

Based on the limitations identified during evaluation, six directions are recommended:

1. *Multi-node cloud deployment:* Although the corrected n = 1 baseline directionally supports hybrid superiority, the CPU-bounded multi-node k3d testbed still lacks production network latency and contention. Deployment on a multi-node cloud cluster (3+ nodes on AWS EKS or GCP GKE with Knative on separate nodes) would validate the results under production network conditions. It is the appropriate setting for the n = 5 confirmatory replication.

2. *Adaptive forecast horizon:* The 9-step GRU horizon deployed in the paired experiments covers provisioning delays up to 120 s. This was enough for the ClarkNet workload. Future work should make the horizon fully adaptive. It should follow the self-calibrating approach of ADAPT @adapt2026. It should increase the required forecast steps based on a live EWMA of measured provisioning delays. The horizon would then track real cluster conditions instead of a fixed step count.

3. *Dynamic workload experiments with extended ramp periods:* PREDICTIVE requires a healthy observation window longer than the ramp duration to build trend predictions. Future experiments should use workload patterns with gradual ramps (90+ seconds) and longer baseline periods.

4. *Predictor improvements beyond the window:* the deployment model now trains on the trace it serves, with the replay window held out. A screening tested longer windows, calendar features, reversible instance normalisation, a log target, a quantile loss, and seed ensembling. The longer window was the only gain, at 4.9% lower error. It lowers a linear autoregression by the same amount. Adopting the 120-sample window under the full study protocol, and testing a linear skip connection, remain future work.

5. *Knative minScale configuration:* Setting minScale=1 would maintain a warm serverless instance. This would remove cold start latency and isolate the routing mechanism's contribution to performance.

6. *Extended statistical validation:* H2 rests on 25 pooled pairs showing no clear win. H1 remains a single n = 1 diagnostic. Future work should run n = 20+ replications to test H1 inferentially and tighten the H2 interval, plus cloud deployment with production network conditions.
